README

This sample demonstrates the software reset library in addon/softreset. The
Raspberry Pi can be rebooted from four different sources:

1. Keyboard: Press Ctrl+Alt+Del on an USB keyboard. The keyboard can be
   attached at any time (USB plug-and-play is enabled).

2. GPIO: Connect a push button between GPIO17 (header pin 11) and GND. The
   internal pull-up resistor is enabled. Hold the button for 2 seconds to reboot.
   The input must have been released once after boot, before it can trigger a
   reset, so that a stuck button does not lead to a reset loop.

3. Ethernet: Send the UDP command "REBOOT circle" to port 5050. The IP address
   is obtained using DHCP and displayed on the screen. You can use the included
   Python 3 script on your host computer:

	python3 softreset.py 192.168.0.123 circle

   The script prints "OK" when the command has been accepted. You can check if
   the system is alive with:

	python3 softreset.py 192.168.0.123 --ping

   It is also possible to use netcat:

	echo "REBOOT circle" | nc -u -w1 192.168.0.123 5050

4. Serial interface UART10 (115200 baud, 8N1). This is the dedicated 3-pin JST
   debug UART connector between the HDMI ports of the Raspberry Pi 5, which can
   be connected with the Raspberry Pi Debug Probe or an USB-to-serial adapter
   (3.3V levels). On earlier models, where UART10 is not available, UART0 on
   GPIO14/15 (header pins 8/10) is used instead. The device is selected with
   RESET_SERIAL_DEVICE in kernel.cpp. To see the log output on this interface
   too, add "logdev=ttyS11" to cmdline.txt (Raspberry Pi 5).

   a) Type "REBOOT circle" and Enter in a terminal program (e.g. minicom,
      PuTTY). "PING" is answered with "PONG". The typed characters and the
      replies are echoed to the terminal and are displayed on the screen too
      (marked with "serial> ").

   b) Send the magic string "circle-reboot", e.g. with:

	python3 ../../tools/reboottool.py circle-reboot /dev/ttyUSB0 115200

      If you use the serial bootloader (see doc/bootloader.txt), add this line
      to Config.mk. Then "make flash" reboots the running sample into the
      bootloader automatically before a new kernel image is uploaded:

	REBOOTMAGIC = circle-reboot

Before the system reboots, a reset handler of the application is called (here
it only switches off the ACT LED), which can be used to bring the application
into a safe state. The GPIO pin, hold time, UDP port, magic string and passwords can be
modified at the top of kernel.cpp.

The addon/softreset library has to be built first:

	cd addon/softreset
	make

Then build this sample:

	cd sample/53-softreset
	make

Please note that the network and serial reset commands are not encrypted. Use it in trusted
networks only.
